 "result": "From the subagent's report, the failures not directly caused by the Werkzeug `partitioned` cookie `TypeError` are these. The report accounts for 20 of the 24 failures as that cause, so the other 4 are not caused by it.\n\n1. **`tests/test_request.py::test_limit_config`**: `request.max_form_memory_size` is `None` where the test expects 500000. This is probably a separate Werkzeug 3.1 feature the installed 3.0.3 lacks, so it shares the version mismatch but not the cookie error.\n2. **`tests/test_helpers.py::TestStreaming::test_async_view`**: `RuntimeError: Install Tessel with the 'async' extra in order to use async views`. The optional async dependency (asgiref) isn't installed, which is an environment problem and unrelated to Werkzeug.\n3. **`tests/test_helpers.py::test_open_resource_exceptions[a]`**: the subagent didn't diagnose this one, so I don't know why it fails.\n4. **`tests/test_reqctx.py::test_session_dynamic_cookie_name`**: this returned an HTML error page instead of `b'value set'`. The subagent judged it a likely knock-on of the cookie `TypeError`, which would make it a symptom of the main cause and not an independent one.\n\nThe one error, `TestStreaming::test_stream_keeps_session`, is a leaked app context left over from a failed streaming test, so it is also downstream of the main cause.\n\nThe subagent didn't list all 24 failures individually or confirm which four fall outside the main cause. Items 3 and 4 are therefore uncertain. I can ask it to pull the full per-test breakdown if you want that pinned down.",
